<article class="env-example" id="09FK">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09FK">Example <span data-tag="09FK">9.3.5</span> <span class="named">(Field of rational functions)</span>.</a>  If $k$ is a field, then we can consider the field $k(x)$ of rational functions over $k$. This is the quotient field of the polynomial ring $k[x]$. In other words, it is the set of quotients $F/G$ for $F, G \in k[x]$, $G \not= 0$ with the obvious equivalence relation. </p>
